NATURAL FAIRIFICATION ORDER (a guide, not a rigid template — skip any category
with no open gap in it, and never invent a step for something not listed above)
1. Identifiers — giving the dataset and its records permanent, unique IDs
2. Documentation — data dictionaries, provenance, describing what the data means
3. Standards and vocabularies — using recognized formats/terminologies instead of ad hoc ones
4. Hosting — where and how the data and its record are stored and kept findable
5. Sharing and access — license, access process, who can get it and how

YOUR TASK
Group the open gaps above into 3-6 ordered steps following that natural order
(merge multiple gaps into one step when they belong to the same category; skip
categories with nothing open). Every gap listed above must be addressed by
exactly one step — do not drop any, do not invent gaps not listed.

OUTPUT FORMAT — follow this exactly, no other text before or after:

GOAL: <one plain sentence: what doing all of this achieves and why it matters
for {{ subject_label }}>
STEP: <short imperative title, e.g. "Give your dataset a permanent identifier">
ADDRESSES: <comma-separated indicator ids from the list above that this step covers>
DETAIL: <1-2 plain sentences on what to actually do, grounded in the gaps this
step addresses>
STEP: <next step>
ADDRESSES: <ids>
DETAIL: <...>

RULES
- Never use an acronym or technical term without explaining it in plain words
  the moment you first use it. Assume zero prior data-management knowledge.
- Do not mention "FAIR principles," indicator codes, or RDA jargon anywhere.
- ADDRESSES must only ever contain ids copied exactly from the OPEN GAPS list
  above — never invent, abbreviate, or guess an id.
- Plain language, second person ("you"). GOAL and each DETAIL: max 40 words.
